<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Pope Cornelius</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">d. 253</span> · <a href="/centuries/3rd-century">3rd century</a> · Also known as Saint Cornelius</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Cornelius was <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bishop">bishop</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a> from 251 until his death in exile at Centumcellae in 253, and is venerated as a saint and martyr. His election was opposed by Novatian, who led a rigorist schism over the readmission of Christians who had lapsed during the Decian persecution. He is commemorated together with his ally <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cyprian">Cyprian of Carthage</a>, and relics of his were brought to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kornelimunster">Kornelimünster</a> near <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aachen">Aachen</a> in the 9th century.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/pope-cornelius.jpg" alt="Pope Cornelius" width="320" height="547" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Hugo DK · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Pope_Cornelius_%E2%80%93_Santa_Maria_in_Trastevere.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
